    x5  ICMP t0/c0 10.70.70.1   -> 192.168.10.5  [ours]   <- ECHO REPLY, source = swi_b's loopback
    x5  ICMP t8/c0 192.168.10.5 -> 10.90.90.1    [ours]
    x5  ICMP t0/c0 10.90.90.1   -> 192.168.10.5  [ours]   <- ECHO REPLY, source = swi_a's loopback
    x5  ICMP t8/c0 192.168.10.5 -> 203.0.113.9   [ours]
    x5  ICMP t8/c0 192.168.10.5 -> 192.168.10.1  [ours]
    x5  ICMP t0/c0 192.168.10.1 -> 192.168.10.5  [ours]
    x2  ICMP t3/c0 192.168.10.1 -> 192.168.10.5  [ours]   <- unreachable FROM THE DUT (the control)
    x1  ARP-REQ who-has 192.168.10.5 tell 192.168.10.1  /  x1 ARP-REPLY (the responder)

    10.70.70.1   (SUBJECT-B)  : 5 sent, **5 echo replies** (0/5 at baseline)
    10.90.90.1   (SUBJECT-A)  : 5 sent, **5 echo replies** (0/5 at baseline)
    203.0.113.9  (CONTROL)    : 5 sent, **0 echo replies**  -- dropped in the SAME capture
    192.168.10.1 (INSTRUMENT) : 5 sent, **5 echo replies**

  The replies' SOURCE addresses are the far-side attribution: 10.70.70.1 is an address that
  exists only on swi_b's loopback and 10.90.90.1 only on swi_a's, so each reply is proof the
  frame reached that specific partner and came back.  The control, sent in the same 20-frame
  leg with the same next-hop MAC, got nothing -- so the DUT is making a per-destination routing
  decision, not flooding.

  STEP 4 VERDICT: PASS -- 0/5 -> 5/5 on both BGP-learned prefixes, measured in the dataplane,
  with an unrouted control at 0/5 and the instrument control at 5/5 in the same capture, and
  with no static route anywhere on the DUT.


STEP 5 -- ADMINISTRATIVE DISTANCE: A STATIC ROUTE DISPLACES THE eBGP ROUTE **IN THE DATAPLANE**
------------------------------------------------------------
Objective: prove the route really is being chosen as a BGP route at AD 20, by displacing it
with a static route at AD 1 and showing the EGRESS MOVE on the wire -- not by reading a number
out of `show ip route`.  Group B measured the same rule for floating statics
(platforms/x960.md 7.4); group C measured it for OSPF at AD 110.

  Applied 22:57:5x (tools/cfg.py on /dev/u2), deliberately pointing at the WRONG partner:
    awplus(config)# ip route 10.70.70.1/32 10.0.41.2        <- 10.0.41.2 is swi_a, which does
                                                               NOT own 10.70.70.1

  Read back (22:57:57-22:57:59):
    awplus# show ip route 10.70.70.1
    Routing entry for 10.70.70.1/32
      Known via "static", distance 1, metric 0, best
      * via 10.0.41.2, vlan41 weight 1          <- the FIB route is now the static, via vlan41

    awplus# show ip bgp 10.70.70.1/32
    BGP routing table entry for 10.70.70.1/32
    Paths: (1 available, best #1, table Default-IP-Routing-Table)
      Advertised to non peer-group peers:
      10.0.41.2
      65002
        10.0.61.2 from 10.0.61.2 (2.2.2.2)
          Origin IGP metric 0, localpref 100, valid, external, best
          Last update: Thu Oct 15 09:52:52 2026

  ⚠️ NOTE FOR THE NEXT TESTER: the BGP path is STILL marked `best` inside `show ip bgp` while
  the FIB is using the static.  Those are two different "best": BGP's own path selection picks
  the best BGP path, and the RIB then compares that path's AD (20) with every other protocol's.
  A tester reading `show ip bgp` alone would conclude BGP is still forwarding.  It is not.
  (Also note `Advertised to non peer-group peers: 10.0.41.2` -- the DUT re-advertises what it
  learned from swi_b onward to swi_a.  That is normal eBGP transit and it matters below.)

  TRAP AVOIDED: `show ip route database 10.70.70.1` is **`% Invalid input detected`** on this
  build -- `show ip route database` takes no address argument here, unlike `show ip route <a>`.

  Dataplane leg (tools/arpleg.py, same four flows, same arguments as step 4 except --tag
  L3-static-over-bgp).  cap L3-static-over-bgp-eth3.pcap: 39 frames

    x5  ICMP t8/c0  192.168.10.5 -> 10.70.70.1
    x5  ICMP **t11/c0  10.0.41.2 -> 192.168.10.5**          <- TIME EXCEEDED, source = swi_a
    x5  ICMP t8/c0  192.168.10.5 -> 10.90.90.1
    x5  ICMP t0/c0  10.90.90.1   -> 192.168.10.5            <- SUBJECT-A still 5/5
    x5  ICMP t8/c0  192.168.10.5 -> 203.0.113.9   (0 replies)
    x5  ICMP t8/c0  192.168.10.5 -> 192.168.10.1
    x5  ICMP t0/c0  192.168.10.1 -> 192.168.10.5            <- instrument 5/5
    x2  ICMP t3/c0  192.168.10.1 -> 192.168.10.5            <- the control's unreachables, from the DUT

    10.70.70.1   (SUBJECT-B)  : 5 sent, **0 echo replies, and 5 ICMP TIME-EXCEEDED from 10.0.41.2**
    10.90.90.1   (SUBJECT-A)  : 5 sent, 5 echo replies
    203.0.113.9  (CONTROL)    : 5 sent, 0 echo replies
    192.168.10.1 (INSTRUMENT) : 5 sent, 5 echo replies

  🔴 THE ATTRIBUTION, and it is strong.  Every one of the five frames came back as ICMP type 11
  **sourced from 10.0.41.2, which is swi_a's vlan41 address**.  Before the static route they
  came back as echo replies from 10.70.70.1 (swi_b).  So the traffic demonstrably left by a
  DIFFERENT PORT, to a DIFFERENT DEVICE.  Type 11 (TTL exceeded) rather than type 3 because the
  DUT re-advertises 10.70.70.1/32 to swi_a over eBGP, so swi_a's own best route for it points
  back at the DUT: the frames loop DUT->swi_a->DUT until the TTL runs out, and swi_a is the hop
  that zeroes it.  A transient micro-loop caused by a deliberately bogus static is the expected
  consequence, not a defect -- and it is excellent evidence, because only swi_a can source it.
  5 of 5, not rate-limited (host/TTL responses are not, unlike net-unreachables -- 7.8).

  Static removed again (22:58:51) and the BGP route returned, proven on the wire:
    awplus(config)# no ip route 10.70.70.1/32 10.0.41.2
    awplus# show ip route 10.70.70.1
    Routing entry for 10.70.70.1/32
      Known via "bgp", distance 20, metric 0, best
      * via 10.0.61.2, vlan61
    awplus# show running-config | include ip route   ->  (EMPTY again)

    leg L4-static-removed, cap 37 frames:
    10.70.70.1   (SUBJECT-B)  : 5 sent, **5 echo replies** (back from swi_b)
    10.90.90.1   (SUBJECT-A)  : 5 sent, 5 echo replies
    203.0.113.9  (CONTROL)    : 5 sent, 0 echo replies
    192.168.10.1 (INSTRUMENT) : 5 sent, 5 echo replies

  STEP 5 VERDICT: PASS -- eBGP administrative distance 20 behaves as documented: a static at
  AD 1 takes the prefix out of the FIB (egress port MOVES, attributed on the wire by ICMP
  sourced from the other partner), and removing the static hands it straight back to BGP.


STEP 6 -- THE PROTOCOL ITSELF, DECODED ON THE WIRE
------------------------------------------------------------
Objective: prove that what carried these prefixes was **BGP-4**, and not something else that
happens to produce a route with the letter B beside it.  No `show` command can do this (group C
technique note).  Two instruments were tried.

6a. 🔴 PORT MIRROR -- TRIED, PRODUCED NOTHING, AND IS A FOURTH INSTANCE OF THE platforms/x960.md
    SECTION 5 MIRRORING DEFECT CLASS.  **No verdict in this case rests on it.**
    Section 5 was read BEFORE the mirror was applied, so `direction both` (defect M3) was
    avoided and the destination port was bounced after applying (the documented working method).
      awplus(config)# interface port1.0.48
      awplus(config-if)# mirror interface port1.0.16 direction receive
      awplus(config-if)# shutdown / no shutdown
      awplus# show mirror
      Mirror Test Port Name: port1.0.48
      Mirror option: Enabled
      Mirror direction: receive
      Monitored Port Name: port1.0.16
      awplus# show interface port1.0.48 status  ->  port1.0.48 connected vlan 48 a-full a-1000
    `tcpdump -i eth4 "tcp port 179 or icmp"` ran across a window in which leg
    L5-mirror-instrument demonstrably pushed traffic through the monitored port -- 5 echo
    requests to 10.70.70.1 and **5 echo replies back, which INGRESS port1.0.16** and are exactly
    what `direction receive` must copy.  Result: **the mirror capture was 0 bytes.  Not one
    frame.**  The instrument-control flow in the same leg (192.168.10.1, 5/5) and the subject
    flow (10.70.70.1, 5/5) both prove the traffic existed.
    ⇒ `show mirror` read Enabled, with the right direction, source and destination, while the
    dataplane copied nothing -- the signature of M1/M2/M3 in platforms/x960.md 5, here on
    `direction receive` on a freshly applied, freshly bounced mirror.  Recorded as an
    OBSERVATION extending section 5; this is NOT a BGP finding and NOT part of T43858's verdict.
    The limb that depended on it is UNMEASURED by that instrument, and was then measured by 6b.

    🔴 AND IT COST SOMETHING -- A TOOL/PLATFORM TRAP WORTH CARRYING FORWARD (two parts):
      (i) `interface <mirror-destination-port>` emits the advisory
          `% port1.0.48 is currently configured as a mirror-port. Use caution when altering its
          config`.  console.ADVISORY does not know that string, so cfg.py treats it as an error;
          worse, the following `shutdown` produced interleaved asynchronous console lines
          (`switch: port 48(port1.0.48) entered disabled state`) that broke prompt detection, and
          cfg.py stopped after 90 s **WITH THE PORT STILL SHUT** -- `no shutdown` never ran.
          Caught immediately by reading the batch output rather than the exit code.  Recovered
          with ckcon.py (which does not stop on `%`) and CKTO=120.
      (ii) After `no mirror interface port1.0.16`, `show interface port1.0.48 status` read
          **Vlan 1**, not Vlan 48.  Configuring the port as a mirror destination took it out of
          its access VLAN and removing the mirror did not put it back.  Restored explicitly with
          `switchport access vlan 48`, verified `connected / vlan 48 / Designated Forwarding`,
          and `show running-config interface port1.0.48` is back to
          `switchport / switchport mode access / switchport access vlan 48` with no mirror and
          no shutdown.  **The underlay was fully restored before any further measurement.**

6b. ✅ THE DUT'S OWN `tcpdump` -- THIS IS THE INSTRUMENT TO USE ON THIS PLATFORM.
    AW+ has `tcpdump` at privileged exec (`tcpdump ?` -> `LINE  tcpdump options`, `vrf`, `<cr>`),
    so BGP, which is unicast TCP between two router addresses and therefore invisible to a host
    NIC on another segment, can be captured where it actually runs.  The session reset was
    triggered from **swi_b's** console while the DUT's console ran the capture -- two different
    devices, one driver each, never two drivers on one /dev/uN.

    (1) THE OPEN EXCHANGE (`clear ip bgp 10.0.61.1` on swi_b; DUT:
        `tcpdump -i vlan61 -c 14 -nn -v tcp port 179`):
          10.0.61.2.59396 > 10.0.61.1.179: Flags [S] ...
          10.0.61.1.179 > 10.0.61.2.59396: Flags [S.] ...
          10.0.61.1.50428 > 10.0.61.2.179: Flags [S] ...        <- BOTH ends initiate: a textbook
                                                                   BGP connection collision
          10.0.61.2.59396 > 10.0.61.1.179: length 53: BGP
            Open Message (1), length: 53
              Version 4, my AS 65002, Holdtime 90s, ID 2.2.2.2
              Optional parameters, length: 24
                Option Capabilities Advertisement (2), length: 6
                  Multiprotocol Extensions (1), length: 4
                    AFI IPv4 (1), SAFI Unicast (1)
                ...
                  32-Bit AS Number (65), length: 4
                     4 Byte AS 65002
          10.0.61.1.179 > 10.0.61.2.59396: length 72: BGP
            Open Message (1), length: 53
              Version 4, my AS 65001, Holdtime 90s, ID 1.1.1.1
              ... AFI IPv4 (1), SAFI Unicast (1) ... 4 Byte AS 65001
            Keepalive Message (4), length: 19
        ⇒ **Version 4** -- this is BGP-4, which is what the case is named for -- the DUT's own
        AS 65001 and router-id 1.1.1.1 on the wire, the default **Holdtime 90 s**, the IPv4
        Unicast AFI/SAFI capability and the 4-byte-AS capability.  TCP port 179, TTL 1 (direct
        eBGP peering), TOS 0xc0 (CS6 internetwork control).

    (2) THE RESET ITSELF, in protocol terms:
          10.0.61.2.179 > 10.0.61.1.49698: length 21: BGP
            Notification Message (3), length: 21, Cease (6), subcode Other Configuration Change (6)
          ... then FIN, then a fresh TCP SYN.
        ⇒ `clear ip bgp <neighbor>` is a **hard reset**: an explicit NOTIFICATION with cease
        subcode "Other Configuration Change", the TCP connection closed, and a new one opened.
        Not a silent drop.  Measured recovery, from `show ip bgp summary` either side of it:
        session Established again in **~6 s**, prefix re-received and back in the FIB within
        **~24 s**, while the OTHER neighbour's session showed Up/Down 00:11:29 straight through
        -- an untouched live control for the reset.

    (3) THE UPDATE THAT CARRIES THE PREFIX (captured during step 7's re-advertisement; DUT
        `tcpdump -i vlan61 -c 1 -nn -v tcp port 179 and greater 90`):
          10:11:39.461215  10.0.61.2.50078 > 10.0.61.1.179 ... length 48: BGP
            Update Message (2), length: 48
              Origin (1), length: 1, Flags [T]: IGP
              AS Path (2), length: 6, Flags [T]: 65002
              Next Hop (3), length: 4, Flags [T]: 10.0.61.2
              Updated routes:
                10.70.70.1/32
        ⇒ **This is the single most direct piece of evidence in the case.**  10.70.70.1/32
        reached the DUT inside a BGP UPDATE message, with ORIGIN=IGP, AS_PATH={65002} and
        NEXT_HOP=10.0.61.2.  No `show` command can produce that, and no other protocol was
        running that could have carried it.

    (4) THE WITHDRAW (captured during step 7's withdrawal, same filter):
          10:13:05.464158  10.0.61.2.50078 > 10.0.61.1.179 ... length 28: BGP
            Update Message (2), length: 28
              Withdrawn routes:
                10.70.70.1/32
              End-of-Rib Marker (empty NLRI)
        ⇒ BGP WITHDRAWS EXPLICITLY.  Quoted verbatim; the "End-of-Rib Marker" line is tcpdump's
        label for an UPDATE carrying no NLRI and no path attributes and should not be read as a
        separate message.  🔴 Contrast with RIP on this same bench (platforms/x960.md 7A.5),
        where a withdrawal is a POISON at metric 16 followed by a 120 s garbage-collect timer.
        BGP needs neither: one UPDATE and the prefix is gone.

    BPF NOTE for the next run: on this tcpdump, `greater N` counts the 14-byte Ethernet header,
    so a 19-byte BGP Keepalive is an 85-byte frame.  `greater 75` still matches keepalives;
    **`greater 90`** is the threshold that isolates UPDATEs (announce 114 B, withdraw 94 B)
    from keepalives and bare ACKs.  That one number turns a noisy capture into a one-packet one.

  STEP 6 VERDICT: PASS -- BGP-4 proven as the protocol on the wire (Version 4 OPEN, the UPDATE
  carrying the subject prefix with its AS_PATH, the explicit withdraw, and the NOTIFICATION on
  reset), using the DUT's own tcpdump.  The port-mirror sub-limb is UNMEASURED and is recorded
  as a platform observation, not as part of this verdict.


STEP 7 -- WITHDRAWAL: THE TIGHTEST CONTROL THE BENCH ALLOWS
------------------------------------------------------------
Objective: withdraw ONE prefix and prove the traffic stops -- with the link, the port, the TCP
session and the BGP adjacency all demonstrably still up, and a second BGP prefix from a second
neighbour still forwarding in the SAME capture.

  Withdrawn on swi_b at 10:10:05-10:10:14 UTC (tools/cfg.py on /dev/u3):
    x960-28YSQ(config)# router bgp 65002
    x960-28YSQ(config-router)# no network 10.70.70.1/32

  Control plane on the DUT at 10:10:48-10:10:51:
    awplus# show ip bgp summary
    Neighbor        V         AS   MsgRc  MsgSnt TblVer InOutQ Up/Down State/PfxRcd
    10.0.41.2       4      65003      44      46      9 0/0    00:17:36        1
    10.0.61.2       4      65002      55      53      9 0/0    **00:01:01        0**
      -> the swi_b session is STILL ESTABLISHED (Up/Down counting up, not reset), it is simply
         receiving 0 prefixes.  The withdrawal did not drop the session.
    awplus# show ip bgp
       Network          Next Hop            Metric LocPrf Weight Path
    *> 10.90.90.1/32    10.0.41.2                0             0 65003 i
    Number of prefixes 1
      -> 10.70.70.1/32 is GONE FROM THE BGP TABLE ENTIRELY.  (Contrast RIP, where the withdrawn
         prefix sits in `show ip rip database` at metric 16 for 120 s.)
    awplus# show ip route bgp   ->  only B 10.90.90.1/32
    awplus# show spanning-tree brief | include port1.0.16
      port1.0.16  0000:e01aead47043  8398  Designated  Forwarding    <- port still forwarding

  Dataplane leg L6-withdrawn (same four flows, same arguments).  cap: 34 frames
    10.70.70.1   (SUBJECT-B)  : 5 sent, **0 echo replies**   (5/5 in step 4)
    10.90.90.1   (SUBJECT-A)  : 5 sent, **5 echo replies**   <- LIVE POSITIVE CONTROL, same capture
    203.0.113.9  (CONTROL)    : 5 sent, 0 echo replies
    192.168.10.1 (INSTRUMENT) : 5 sent, 5 echo replies
  Note there is NOT ONE frame of 10.70.70.1 traffic in the capture other than our five requests
  -- no replies, and no ICMP from swi_b.  The DUT dropped them itself.

  🔴 WHY THIS LEG IS TRUSTWORTHY.  The zero on SUBJECT-B is measured against three positives in
  the same 20-frame capture: a second BGP prefix from a different neighbour over a different
  port (5/5), the DUT's own address (5/5), and an unrouted control that is zero in every leg.
  Link, port, STP state, TCP session and BGP adjacency were all read as up immediately before.
  The only thing that changed was one `network` statement on the far end.

  RE-ADVERTISED at 10:13:25 UTC.  Measured recovery (10:13:41):
    awplus# show ip route bgp
    B       10.70.70.1/32 [20/0] via 10.0.61.2, vlan61, **00:00:04**
    B       10.90.90.1/32 [20/0] via 10.0.41.2, vlan41, 00:20:28
    awplus# show ip bgp summary  ->  10.0.61.2 ... 00:03:56  1     (session never reset)
  i.e. the route was back in the FIB within ~12 s of the command being typed on a 9600-baud
  console, and the UPDATE that did it is decoded in step 6b(3).

  Dataplane leg L7-readvertised.  cap: 37 frames
    10.70.70.1   (SUBJECT-B)  : 5 sent, **5 echo replies**
    10.90.90.1   (SUBJECT-A)  : 5 sent, 5 echo replies
    203.0.113.9  (CONTROL)    : 5 sent, 0 echo replies
    192.168.10.1 (INSTRUMENT) : 5 sent, 5 echo replies

  (The withdraw/re-advertise cycle was run three times in all, to capture the announce UPDATE
  and then the withdraw UPDATE with the corrected BPF threshold.  Every cycle behaved
  identically; the counted legs above are the first withdrawal and the final re-advertisement.)

  STEP 7 VERDICT: PASS -- one prefix withdrawn, 5/5 -> 0/5 on the wire, with a second BGP prefix
  from a second neighbour at 5/5 in the same capture and the session still Established; and the
  prefix restored to 5/5 within ~12 s of being re-advertised.
